Step 0 points away from a Track A claim. The paper proves existence in a high-semi-degree regime but gives no average-case hardness result; its proof is constructive (short-path construction, random partitioning, pinch vertices, then Hamilton-cycle machinery). I’m therefore pursuing Track B on the paper’s native special case \(H=\) one loop: a rooted directed Hamilton cycle in a regular oriented Cayley tournament. The general tournament algorithm is polynomial and will be disclosed and measured; the intended shortcut is a hidden change of coordinates that turns the cycle into modular translation.
