codex
Implemented and locally verified a Track B rooted Hamilton-loop subdivision generator based on the paper’s native oriented-graph objects.

Files:

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2407.06675/gen_2407_06675.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2407.06675/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2407.06675/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2407.06675/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2407.06675/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2407.06675/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local results:

- All nine local gates pass.
- Guessing: 0/200,000 over a \(46!\) structure-aware space.
- Four attacks: 0/8 each.
- Reference Camion algorithm: 8/8, 10,787 operations on the reporting instance.
- Compact centered-inversion route: at most 278 operations.
- Answer: 135 characters, 48 atoms.
- Canonical-key transformations: 100/100; unrelated keys: 20/20.

The family is derived from the paper’s one-loop \(H\)-subdivision case and uses regular oriented tournaments satisfying its numerical semi-degree inequality. See the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.06675).

Step 4 remains externally blocked: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” for every bare, hinted, and placebo call. The transcripts honestly record these as errors, and the submission validator stops at the missing multi-vendor hardness evidence. A replenished `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is required before submission; all three oracle arms must then be rerun.
