/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:376:\begin{lemma} \label{sublemma1}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:381:\begin{lemma}[Short-path existence lemma]\label{lemma3}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:385:Let \(D\) be an oriented graph on \(n\) vertices with \(\delta^0(D) \geq n/3 + 6\), and let \(x, y \in V(D)\) be distinct vertices. We aim to construct an \((x,y)\)-path of length \(l\).
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:387:For \(l \leq 6\), by the minimum semi-degree condition, we can construct a path \(Q\) of length \(l-4 \leq 2\) from \(x\) to some vertex \(v\) avoiding \(y\). Applying Lemma \ref{le3} to \((D \setminus V(Q)) \cup \{v\}\), we find a \((v,y)\)-path of length 4, which combined with \(Q\) yields an \((x,y)\)-path of length \(l\).
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:389:For \(l \geq 7\) and \(n \geq 10^{10}l\), by Fact \ref{fac2}, there exists an \(xu\)-butterfly {with vertex set $\{a,b,z,x,u\}$} for some vertex \(u \in V(D) \setminus \{y\}\). We greedily construct a path \(P\) of length \(l-7\) from \(u\) to some vertex \(v\), avoiding \(a, b, z, x, y\). The minimum semi-degree condition ensures the existence of such a path. Next, we apply Lemma \ref{sublemma1} to \(D - (\{a, b, z, x, u\} \cup (V(P) \setminus \{v\}))\) with \(C = l-7\), {$s:=v$ and $t:=y$}. Since \(n \geq 10^{10}l\), the conditions \(n - l - 2 \geq 8 \cdot 10^9 C\) and $\delta ^0(D  \setminus (\{a, b, z, x, u\} \cup V(P))\cup \{v\})\geq (n-C)/3-C+1 $ hold, and thus we obtain a \((v,y)\)-path of length 3,4 or 5. Finally, we select a path from \(x\) to \(u\) in the \(xu\)-butterfly of appropriate length \(k \in \{2, 3, 4\}\) such that \(k + (l - 7) + m = l\), where \(m \in \{3, 4, 5\}\) is the length of the \((v,y)\)-path. This completes the construction of an \((x,y)\)-path of length \(l\).
