/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:424:\begin{lemma}\label{chva} (Chv\'{a}tal \cite{chva}). Let
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:428:denote the probability that a random subset of $k$ elements out of $n, \gamma n$ of which are marked, contains at most $(\gamma-\delta) k$ marked elements; then
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:434:In \cite{Alon(1996)}, Alon and Fischer gave a random partition of a graph such that each vertex has a proportional distribution of neighbours in each part. In \cite{sudakov(2009)}, Keevash and Sudakov provided the corresponding digraph version. Lemma \ref{key1} further generalizes these results by offering a random partition of a large subset $U$ such that each vertex with large neighbours in $U$ has a proportional distribution of neighbours in each part.
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:436:\begin{lemma}\label{key1}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:441:We randomly choose a partition of the vertices of \(U\) into sets \(A\) and \(B\) with orders \(m\) and \(l\), respectively, such that each possible partition is equally probable. Let \(v\) be a vertex in \(V(D)\) with \(d^+_{U}(v) \geq \beta |U|\). Applying Lemma \ref{chva} with \(n := |U|, \gamma :=\beta, k:=m,\) and \( \delta:=n^{-1/3}\), the probability that fewer than \(\left(\beta - n^{-1 / 3}\right) m\) out-neighbours of \(v\) lie in \(A\) is at most \(\mathrm{e}^{-2 n^{-2 / 3} m} \leq \mathrm{e}^{-2 \varepsilon \beta n^{1 / 3}}\leq \mathrm{e}^{-2 \varepsilon ^2  n^{1 / 3}}\). A similar estimate applies to the out-neighbours of \(v\) in \(B\). Thus, the probability that any of these conditions is not satisfied for some vertex in \(V(D)\) is at most \(2 n \mathrm{e}^{-2 \varepsilon^2 n^{1 / 3}}\). We can choose \(n_0\) such that for \(n > n_0\), this probability is less than \(1/2\), ensuring the existence of the required partition.
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:444:By repeatedly applying Lemma \ref{key1}, we can obtain the following more generalized result. This provides a random partition of a digraph such that each vertex has a proportional distribution of in- and out-neighbours in each part.
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:447:\begin{lemma}\label{lemma2}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:462:In this process, each application of Lemma \ref{key1} introduces an additive error of at most \( n^{2/3} \) in the number of out-neighbours (resp., in-neighbours) for any vertex \( v \). Since the partition is constructed via \( s - 1 \) iterations, the total error accumulates to \( 2(s - 1)n^{2/3} \), which is bounded by \( 2sn^{2/3} \). We thus verify (ii). For any vertex \( v \) with \( d^+_{V_l}(v) \geq \beta |V_l| \), the number of out-neighbours of $v$ in each \( S^i_l \) is at least $\beta m^i_l - 2sn^{2/3}.$ Similarly, for in-neighbours with \( d^-_{V_l}(v) \geq \beta |V_l| \), the same bound holds.
