/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:691:\begin{definition}\emph{(Robust $(\mu,\tau)$-outexpander)} \label{def1}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:698:\begin{lemma}\label{4.4}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:719:Lemma \ref{4.5} gives a sufficient condition for Hamiltonicity of oriented graphs.
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:721:\begin{lemma}\label{4.5}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:722:\cite{Keevash(2009)} Let $M',n_0$ be positive numbers and let $\varepsilon,d,\eta, \nu,\tau$ be positive constants such that $1/n_0\ll 1/M'\ll \varepsilon \ll d\ll \mu \ll \tau \ll \eta <1$. Let $D$ is an oriented graph on $n\geq n_0$ vertices such that $\delta^0(D)\geq 2\eta n$. Let $R'$ be the reduced digraph of $D$ with parameters $(\varepsilon, d)$ and $|R'|\geq M'$. If $R'$ has a spanning oriented subgraph $R$ with $\delta^0(R)\geq \eta |R|$ such that $R$ is a robust $(\mu, \tau)$-outexpander, then $D$ contains a Hamilton cycle.
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:728:\begin{definition}[Pinch vertex operation]\label{keydef}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:729:Given an oriented graph $D$, two vertices \(u, v \in V(D)\), and two subsets \(X, Y \subseteq V(D)\): If \(N_X^+(u) \cap N_Y^-(v) \neq \emptyset\), partition \(N_X^+(u) \cap N_Y^-(v)\) into two sets \(N_u, N_v\) of size as equal as possible; Otherwise, set \(N_u = N_v = \emptyset\). Define \(N_1 = N_X^+(u) \setminus N_v\) and \(N_2 = N_Y^-(v) \setminus N_u\). The pinch vertex operation (on \(u, v\) with respect to \(X, Y\)) constructs an oriented graph \(D_{uv}\) as follows: {$V(D_{uv})=(V(D)\setminus \{u,v\})\cup \{x_{u,v}\}$, where $x_{u,v}$ is a new vertex; $A(D_{uv})=A(D-\{u,v\})\cup \{x_{u,v}w\mid w\in N_1\} \cup \{wx_{u,v}\mid w\in N_2\} $.}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:749:\subsection{Extremal case}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:754:\begin{lemma}\label{4.6}
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:760:\textbf{Overview of Lemma \ref{4.6}.} {Let \(f:V(H)\to V(D)\) be an injection, and let \(B = f(V(H))\) denote the image of $f$. First, according to the extremal partition of $D$ and the small size of $B$, we readily derive an initial extremal partition of \(D \setminus B\). Applying Lemma \ref{4.2} to refine this partition yields a refined extremal partition \((D_1, D_2, D_3, D_4)\) of \(D \setminus B\) such that \(|D_2| = |D_4| + b\), where $b$ is determined by the neighbour distribution of branch vertices.} {The structure of this partition ensures the existence of all short subdivision paths. Apply Lemma \ref{lemma2} to the remaining subdigraph of $D\setminus B$ --- obtained by removing vertices from these short subdivision paths --- to yield $s$ subgraphs $G_1,\ldots,G_s$, where $s$ denotes the number of required long subdivision paths.} Pinch the branch vertices \( u_i \) and \( v_i \) into a single vertex and added to \( G_i \), followed by adjusting a few vertices to ensure \( |V(G_i) \cap D_2| = |V(G_i) \cap D_4| \) (feasible due to \( |D_2| = |D_4| + b \)) and $|G_i|=n_i$. Finally, we verify that each oriented graph $G_{i}$ satisfies the condition of Lemma \ref{4.3}. This implies the existence of a Hamilton cycle in \( G_i \), thereby yielding a subdivision $(u_i,v_i)$-path of length \( n_i \) in \( D \), which completes the proof.
/tmp/arxiv-2407.06675-src-XHnjnv/final-Semi-Degree_Condition_for_Arbitrary_H-Linked.tex:774: Indeed, $b(P_{i})$ denotes that the $(u_i, v_i)$-path that we construct next  use \(b(P_{i})\) more vertices from \(D^*_2\) than in \(D^*_4\). That is, $b(P_{i})=|(V(P_{i})\cap D^*_2|-|V(P_{i})\cap D^*_4)| $. Let $b=\sum_{i\in [q]}b(P_{i}),$ then $|b|\leq q$. W.l.o.g., assume $b\geq 0$. Apply Lemma \ref{4.2}, by adjusting the location of at most $ 65\mu n $ vertices from \( (D^*_1, D^*_2, D^*_3, D^*_4) \) and contracting a collection of disjoint paths \( Q_1, \ldots, Q_r \) with \( \left|\bigcup_{i=1}^r V(Q_i)\right| \leq 650\mu n \), there exists an extremal partition \( (D_1, D_2, D_3, D_4) \) with parameters $ (10^3, 10^6,  1 /400,1-10^6\sqrt{\mu}) $ such that \( |D_2| - |D_4| = b \). It not hard to see that every pair of vertices $u_i,v_i\in B$ still satisfies $d^+_{D_{i^+ }}(u_i)\geq n/100$, $d^-_{D_{i^-}}(v_i)\geq n/100$. In either case, let $C$ be the set of all contraction vertices that {are} obtained by contracting paths \( Q_1, \ldots, Q_r \), so $|C|\leq 650\mu n$.
