The paper’s main theorem is existential, not a computational-hardness result. Its proof routes long prescribed paths through random partitions and Hamilton cycles, while short paths are constructed greedily. I’m now checking whether any native certificate-search task has a genuine Track B gap; the obvious “plant short disjoint paths” idea is already suspect because dense oriented graphs make such paths abundant and greedy search cheap.
